&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the team:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At X, we are researching volumetric light-matter interactions and laser induced material modifications in specialized glass materials. A core technical challenge we are tackling is the development of the material itself. In particular, we are engineering glass compositions that can reliably undergo these transitions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;About the role:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a Resident Research Scientist, you will develop new glass materials. You will be responsible for the end-to-end process, including design, high-temperature synthesis, and rigorous characterization &amp;amp; testing of their optical and structural properties for our application of interest.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This role is designed for active PhD students who want to apply their academic and research expertise in an industrial context. You will work independently, supported by a cross-functional team of optics and hardware engineers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How you will make 10x impact:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 400;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Material synthesis: Design and execute synthesis protocols to generate novel glasses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dopant functionalization: optimize the incorporation of functional dopants to achieve targeted photosensitivity and refractive index modulation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Advanced characterization: utilize analytical tools to evaluate performance: spectroscopic, thermal, and structural (XRD/SEM).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experimental strategy: Design complex experimental matrices (DOE) to identify optimal formulations for laser-induced modification.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cross-functional collaboration: Partner with engineers to test new materials.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;IP generation: Document findings and contribute to the project’s IP portfolio by drafting invention disclosures and technical reports.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you should have:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Actively pursuing a Ph.D. in Materials Science, Chemical Engineering, Chemistry, or a related field.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hands-on experience with high-temperature synthesis and characterization of dielectric inorganic glasses or ceramic materials.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understanding of glass structure-property relationships and how compositional changes affect optical behavior.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It’d be great if you also had these:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Current or prior research focuses on functionalizing transparent glass materials&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience researching laser-induced modifications in glass and/or other transparent materials.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Demonstrated ability to translate fundamental research into scalable, repeatable preparation protocols.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Thriving in ambiguity and a desire to tackle 10X problems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
